he loop; the only thing that ends reading early is the byte budget, which omits sections. \"Per document\" has no present meaning | observed | LH1 (D6) |\n139\t| H9 | #216 \"Request\" | producer protocol, live and replayable; stop rule per document; roster cap and byte budget as parameters; `replay()` against a caller root | inherited | LH1, LH2 |\n140\t| H10 | consumers of `reading.py` | `revision.py:171` calls `reading.replay(retained, fixture=inputs, vocabulary_fixture=inputs)` and `:62-64` reads `reading.FIXTURE` and `reading.DEPTH_FIXTURE`; `assurance_report.py:760` calls `replay(run_dir)` with no root and `:725` caps coverage rows at 128 `(p1)`; the tests patch `SavedProducer.produce`, `_read` (on the replay path, `test_ld3_reading_workflow.py:238-246`) and `_public_results`, call `_inspect` directly with six positional arguments and read `producer.calls` (`:153-165`) `(p1)`, and read `ROSTER_LIMIT` | observed | LH1, LH2 |\n141\t| H11 | probe, 2026-11-22 | no test pins the bytes of a packaged reading run. `fidelity-high.json` and `fidelity-low.json` carry `gnd_version`. Evidence digests at gnd 0.4.0: `full` `ee67e13c…fd1c`, `--scenario unavailable` `d4010f94…6ddf`, `--budget-bytes 0` `7dcf43ec…a071` | observed | LH1 |\n142\t| H12 | #215 \"Problem\" | `project()` (`assertion_admission.py:100`) accepts three fact types only (`:105`); every role is a mandatory non-empty string (`:45-52`); absence is a sentinel (`:30`, `:84`); identity is (work, passage) (`:55-63`, `:111-113`); the admissible pair is hard-coded (`:122`); `MAX_TARGETS` (`:29`) bounds rows (`:46`) and targets (`:67`) | observed | LH3, LH4 |\n143\t| H13 | `src/gnd/judgments.py:115`, `:121` | `gnd judgments check` itself refuses a policy with more than 1,024 targets or more than 4,096 judge-target pairs. A `MAX_TARGETS` parameter can lower the example's bound; it cannot admit more than 1,024 targets in one call. The reporter's gold set has about 3,600 claims | observed | LH4 (D11) |\n144\t| H14 | `src/gnd/model/_validation.py:942`, `model_verbalization.py:124`, `evolve/service.py:577` | `objectified_as` is read by validation, verbalization and evolve only. The checker does not join an objectified fact's row to the entity that objectifies it; that link is the model author's convention | observed | LH3 (D10) |\n145\t| H15 | `assertion_admission.py:146-148`, `:188-191`; include-root-routes plan §1 H11 | the admission snapshot copies one model file, so an include root fails its include read. `gnd judgments check` accepts include roots since IR1 | observed | LH3 |\n146\t| H16 | `assertion_admission.py:35-39`, `:83-88`, `:118`, `:132` | checks that belong to the packaged shape only: the five closed tag vocabularies, the rule that `conditional` needs a condition and `unqualified` forbids one, and the fixed `covers` list | observed | LH3 |\n147\t| H17 | consumer of `assertion_admission.py` | `lineage_run.py:33` imports `ABSENT_CONDITIONS`, `PASSAGE`, `ROLES`, `SCHEMA`, `STATEMENT`, `WORK` and `admit`; `:203` calls `admit` with six positional arguments; `:254` compares the marker's `schema` with `SCHEMA` | observed | LH3, LH4 |\n148\t| H18 | probe, 2026-11-22 | packaged admission output at `4ad0d216`: `admitted.population.json` `6e45b93d…39e1`, `raw.population.json` `ea96584c…0173`, `judgment-report.json` `3726a424…c43c`, `admission.json` `6d262018…f16f`. Statement indices 3, 4, 5 and 6 pass `semantic-support` and fail `positive-own-findings` with `negated`, `baseline-comparison`, `speculation`, `cites-other-work` | observed | LH3, LH4 |\n149\t| H19 | #215, measured shape | 98 % of claims share a passage; about half have other than one actor and one object; 9 % have several conditions; 44 % carry a quantity | inherited | LH3 fixture |\n150\t| H20 | literature-depth close, row \"Host-scale reading and extraction\" | interface half only; the corpus and provider-budget run stays with the literature-identity follow-up | inherited | LH1, LH2 |\n151\t| H21 | PROJECT.md, roadmap after 0.4.0 | third milestone; independent of the two export milestones, both merged | inherited | all |\n152\t| H22 | component index, `scripts/components_index.py` | `examples.literature_reading` and `examples.literature_assertion_admission` exist, both `canonical`; the capabilities below adapt them and add no component. `review` lists seven literature entries whose descriptions are older than their code. The index changes in the same commit as the code | observed | all |\n153\t| H23 | probe, 2026-11-22; `reading.py:283-285`, `:393` | the loop completes only when `verify` and `check` both exit 0 on the broad and the targeted population, and admits only exits 0 and 1 from them. On a copy of the reading model, a population with one `system` fact and no `subject_label` exits 2 (`inadequate`) from both; adding one `subject_label` row gives 0 and 0. `LABEL` is what keeps the packaged model's one constrained fact type populated. A caller model with a constraint over a fact type a pass has not read yet exits 2, and the run refuses \"check exited 2\" | observed | LH1 (D16) |\n154\t| H24 | `docs/reference/rule-check-report.md:85-93`; `checker/_core.py:423`; `reading.py:268-271` | a checking route's exits are 0, 1, 2 and 4, with precedence 1 > 4 > 2 > 0; 4 means a must-hold rule is unchecked. `_route` refuses any exit outside the set it is given `(p1)` | observed | LH1 (D16) |\n155\t| H25 | probe, 2026-11-23; `project.md` lint gate | the whole-repository `uv run ruff check` exited 1 at `4ad0d216` with 198 findings, all in `review_tool.py`, `rules_check.py` and `handoff.py` as delivered from dev-tools (both discovery roots); `project.md` requires it to exit 0. Fixed upstream (dev-tools PR 34, `a29562a`) and re-synced; it exits 0 after the sync `(p1)` | observed | all |\n156\t\n157\tOut of scope: the corpus and provider-budget run (H20); promoting either\n158\tmodule to stable CLI; any change to a stable command or its caps (R0.7, H13);\n159\tthe assurance scorer's own 128-row cap (H10); #218, #221 and #222; release,\n160\ttag and publication.\n161\t\n162\t### Issue requests and the phases that discharge them\n163\t\n164\t| Request | Phase |\n165\t|---|---|\n166\t| #216: a public producer protocol, live and replayable | LH1, LH2 |\n167\t| #216: the stop rule per document as well as global | LH1 |\n168\t| #216: the roster cap and the byte budget as parameters | LH1 |\n169\t| #216: `replay()` against a caller-given input root | LH2 |\n170\t| #215: caller-named assertion entity, identity from its preferred uniqueness | LH3 |\n171\t| #215: every fact row of an admitted assertion kept | LH3 |\n172\t| #215: optional roles are optional | LH3 |\n173\t| #215: polarity and attribution rules as data, with a distinct disposition | LH4 |\n174\t| #215: `MAX_TARGETS` as a parameter | LH4 |\n175\t\n176\tThis is a framework milestone. An experiment-claim census (`gnd coverage`)\n177\tcounts claims over a domain model; it says nothing about which arguments an\n178\texample function accepts, so none is read here. The baseline is the test run\n179\tabove and the probes H6, H11, H18 and H23.\n180\t\n181\t## 2. Contract proposal and product behavior\n182\t\n183\tExact names and signatures are fixed in each phase spec. What follows is the\n184\tcontract each spec must realise.\n185\t\n186\t### LH1 — a live producer over a caller root\n187\t\n188\t- **The protocol.** A producer is any object with\n189\t  `produce(call) -> dict`. `call` is a `ProducerCall`: the stage, the section\n190\t  ID, its source ID and version, its kind and span, the section's source bytes\n191\t  and their sha256, and the open questions that point at the section. The\n192\t  result is the response object the loop accepts today: `outcome` (one of\n193\t  `accepted`, `empty`, `refused`), `facts` and `proposal_keys`. The loop\n194\t  counts calls itself; a producer needs no `calls` attribute. `SavedProducer`\n195\t  is one implementation and keeps its pin and roster checks.\n196\t- **A response is untrusted input.** The loop validates each response as a\n197\t  bounded JSON value of the declared shape before it uses it (standing\n198\t  rule 1). A response that fails, or a producer that raises, refuses the run\n199\t  with nothing written (D7).\n200\t- **A caller root.** A root that holds `profile.json` is a caller root. The\n201\t  profile names the reading model file and its include files, and supplies\n202\t  what the loop takes from constants today: the facts added to every\n203\t  population, the producer's label, and the optional review, calibration,\n204\t  conflict and absence steps. A\n205\t  root without a profile is the packaged layout and keeps the packaged\n206\t  constants. A live producer over a root without a profile refuses. A\n207\t  declared calibration step takes its renderer label and the names of its two\n208\t  case folders from the profile; the packaged label, folder names and count\n209\t  of two are the packaged profile's values (H3) `(p1)`.\n210\t- **Unavailable sections `(p1)`.** On a caller root the caller names its\n211\t  unavailable sections with an argument, and the manifest records them.\n212\t  `scenario=\"unavailable\"` names the packaged section `study-methods`, so\n213\t  over a caller root it refuses before any producer call.\n214\t- **Steps.** A step the profile does not declare is recorded `skipped`\n215\t  (R0.6). A declared step runs on the caller's files. A reviewer, if given,\n216\t  is called once per accepted response and must return a `supported` receipt;\n217\t  any other decision refuses the run, as a stale review does today. The\n218\t  reviewer is a check, not a filter (D3).\n219\t- **Order.** Inputs are validated, then the reading model is extracted, then\n220\t  the first producer call is made (H7, standing rule 3).\n221\t- **Route exits.** On a caller root the exits of `verify` and `check` over\n222\t  the broad and the targeted population are recorded as observed, 0, 1, 2 or\n223\t  4, and the run completes; any other exit refuses (D16, H23, H24) `(p1)`.\n224\t  The report shows the exits; it never calls a population verified. The\n225\t  packaged root keeps requiring 0.\n226\t- **Per document.** `document_budget` bounds the bytes read from each source.\n227\t  It follows the global budget's rule `(p1)`: a section that does not fit in\n228\t  what is left of its document's budget is omitted, and a later, smaller\n229\t  section of the same document is still read (`reading.py:172-174`). Other\n230\t  documents continue. The report states a stop reason for each source, with\n231\t  the three reasons the global stop has. A document whose required questions\n232\t  are resolved still gets its other scheduled reads (D6, ruled `(o)`). It is\n233\t  a `run()` argument beside the global budget, with the largest-budget limit\n234\t  as its ceiling (D4).\n235\t- **Limits.** The roster cap, the largest budget, the per-file input cap, the\n236\t  response cap, the route timeout, the route output cap and the retained-file\n237\t  cap are fields of one limits argument, each with a default equal to\n238\t  today's constant or today's effective bound, and a hard ceiling (D4, R0.8)\n239\t  `(p1)`. A JSON file is decoded under the cap it was read with, so a raised\n240\t  input cap also raises the decode cap of that input; the fixed 1 MiB decode\n241\t  cap of `depth.py:61` no longer applies to the reading loop.\n242\t- **Retained files `(p1)`.** Replay reads a run's own files under the\n243\t  retained-file cap the run recorded. Before the first producer call,\n244\t  `run()` bounds each file it will retain (each payload holds at most its\n245\t  stage's scheduled sections times the response cap) and refuses if a bound\n246\t  exceeds the retained-file cap. A run that completes can therefore always be\n247\t  read back.\n248\t- **What a caller run writes.** The captured response table and the captured\n249\t  review receipts are artifacts of the run. Its manifest has format\n250\t  `literature-reading-run/2` and records the settings, the unavailable\n251\t  sections and each step's status (D5, R0.5). A packaged run with no new\n252\t  argument writes `literature-reading-run/1`, byte for byte (R0.2).\n253\t- In LH1, `replay()` refuses a `/2` run by name; LH2 replaces that refusal.\n254\t\n255\t### LH2 — replay of a live run\n256\t\n257\t- `replay(folder, fixture=ROOT)` reads the manifest, takes the settings and\n258\t  step statuses from it, reads the caller root through its profile, reads\n259\t  the run's files under the recorded retained-file cap `(p1)`, and\n260\t  re-derives every artifact. It has no producer, reviewer, budget or limits\n261\t  argument.\n262\t- It refuses a response table, receipt table, stage, payload or input whose\n263\t  bytes differ from what the manifest binds, and a manifest whose settings\n264\t  exceed a hard ceiling.\n265\t- A manifest binds a run internally. `replay(..., expected_evidence_sha256=)`\n266\t  compares the run with the digest `run()` returned, which the host keeps\n267\t  outside the folder; without it a consistently rewritten folder replays. The\n268\t  README says so. For the packaged run the independent producer pin keeps\n269\t  that role.\n270\t- `score_reading_coverage` gains the same `fixture` argument and passes it\n271\t  through. Its 128-row cap stays (out of scope).\n272\t\n273\t### LH3 — admission over a caller's assertion entity\n274\t\n275\t- **Shape.** The caller passes an assertion shape: the ID of an entity type\n276\t  in its model, and the entity types to follow from it (D17, ruled). With no shape,\n277\t  `admit()` runs the packaged shape and writes today's bytes.\n278\t- **Attached facts.** A fact type is attached when one of its roles is played\n279\t  by the assertion entity. A row belongs to the assertion its role value\n280\t  names. Every attached row of an admitted assertion is kept.\n281\t- **Followed facts `(o)`.** Admission follows links outward (D9, ruled). When\n282\t  a kept row names an instance of a followed entity type, the rows of the\n283\t  fact types that entity plays a role in are kept for that instance too, and\n284\t  the walk continues from them. Example: a claim's row names quantity `Q7`;\n285\t  `Q7`'s value row and unit row are kept. The walk stops at the shape's\n286\t  list (D17, ruled): an instance of an entity type the shape does not list is\n287\t  kept as a value in the row that names it, and its own facts are not copied.\n288\t  With an empty list nothing is followed. One limit holds beside the list: a\n289\t  row that names an assertion which was not admitted is never kept, whichever\n290\t  route reaches it.\n291\t- **Coverage.** The admitted population's `covers` lists the attached fact\n292\t  types and the fact types of the followed entity types. A fact type outside\n293\t  both is not copied.\n294\t- **Identity `(o)`.** The assertion's own handle is its identity (D10,\n295\t  ruled). The model's preferred uniqueness is enforced by the stable\n296\t  commands, not re-implemented (R0.7). No rule links an objectified fact\n297\t  type's rows to the entity (H14): a shape that names an entity which\n298\t  objectifies a fact type refuses, naming the objectification. The packaged\n299\t  model's `assertion` is such an entity, so the packaged example stays on the\n300\t  packaged path.\n301\t- **Judged unit.** Policy targets are rows attached directly to the\n302\t  assertion. An assertion is supported when at least one of its rows is a\n303\t  target and every target among its rows is admitted by\n304\t  `gnd judgments check` (D8). A target on any other row refuses.\n305\t- **Optional facts.** An assertion with no row in an attached fact type\n306\t  simply has none. No value is read as \"absent\". The tag vocabularies and the\n307\t  condition rule of H16 apply to the packaged shape only.\n308\t- **Row bound `(p1)`.** The generic projection bounds the rows it reads,\n309\t  attached and followed together, with its own `max_rows` argument, separate\n310\t  from the target cap (D18). The packaged projection keeps `MAX_TARGETS` as\n311\t  its row bound (`:46`), so its bytes do not move.\n312\t- **Include roots.** The snapshot copies each declared include beside the\n313\t  root, and the record binds each include's sha256 (H15).\n314\t- **Record.** A call with any new argument (a shape, rules, `max_targets` or\n315\t  `max_rows`) writes `literature-assertion-admission/2`; the packaged call\n316\t  with none keeps `/1` (D12). A `/2` record binds the shape and the rules by\n317\t  sha256 of their canonical bytes, and records `max_targets` and `max_rows`\n318\t  `(p1)`. A call with rules and no shape is a packaged-shape `/2` call: it\n319\t  records the shape as `packaged`.\n320\t\n321\t### LH4 — admission rules as data\n322\t\n323\t- **Rules.** The caller passes admission rules: an ordered list of\n324\t  requirements, each with an ID, an attached fact type, a role, the\n325\t  admissible values and, optionally, a reason for each inadmissible value.\n326\t  An assertion satisfies a requirement when it has at least one row of that\n327\t  fact type and every such row has an admissible value. Requirements are\n328\t  evaluated in order, and the first one that fails gives the reason: the\n329\t  reason declared for the offending value, else the requirement's ID `(p1)`.\n330\t- **Default rules `(p1)`.** The packaged comparison at `:122` becomes the\n331\t  default rules, evaluated by the one evaluator (R0.4): first `polarity` with\n332\t  `asserts` admissible and the reason `negated` for `negates`, then\n333\t  `attribution` with `own-finding` admissible and each other value as its own\n334\t  reason. List order keeps today's polarity-first precedence, and the\n335\t  reasons are today's strings, so the H18 stage table does not move.\n336\t- **Disposition.** In a `/2` record, a supported assertion that fails a\n337\t  requirement has status `policy-refused` and that reason; an unsupported\n338\t  one keeps `fail` (D2). The `/1` record keeps today's statuses, so it keeps\n339\t  its bytes.\n340\t- **A rule that names nothing refuses.** A requirement naming an unknown fact\n341\t  type or role refuses the call. It is never evaluated as \"no row matched\".\n342\t- **Target cap.** `max_targets` is an argument from 1 to 1,024. The ceiling\n343\t  is the stable command's (H13, D11).\n344\t\n345\t## 3. Dependencies and artifact homes\n346\t\n347\t| Phase | Needs | Homes it changes |\n348\t|---|---|---|\n349\t| LH1 | nothing | `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/depth.py` (one keyword on `_command`), a new `tests/test_lh1_reading_host.py`, a new `tests/fixtures/lh1-reading/` (the caller root and the recorded packaged trees), `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading_fixture/README.md`, `dev/components/` |\n350\t| LH2 | LH1 | `reading.py`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/assurance_report.py` (one pass-through argument), a new `tests/test_lh2_reading_replay.py`, the same README, `docs/literature/commands.md`, `docs/release-notes.md`, `dev/components/` |\n351\t| LH3 | nothing | `src/gnd/examples/literature/assertion_admission.py`, a new `tests/test_lh3_admission_shape.py`, a new `tests/fixtures/lh3-admission/`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/README.md`, `dev/components/` |\n352\t| LH4 | LH3 | `assertion_admission.py`, a new `tests/test_lh4_admission_rules.py`, `tests/fixtures/lh3-admission/` (rules files), the same README, `docs/literature/commands.md`, `docs/release-notes.md`, `dev/components/` |\n353\t\n354\tOrder: LH1, LH2, LH3, LH4, one at a time on the branch\n355\t`claude/literature-host-interfaces`. The graph is acyclic. The two pairs share\n356\tno source file; they run in sequence so that `docs/literature/commands.md`,\n357\t`docs/release-notes.md` and `dev/components/` have one writer at a time. Start\n358\tcondition: `main` at `4ad0d216`, which holds IR1, so LH3's fixture can be an\n359\tinclude root